  "updated": "2026-07-20T18:21:53.439926+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...More strong storms possible especially Tuesday.\n\nWeak front situated from the Delmarva to the NC foothills will\nfocus convection this afternoon with best coverage in\nassociation withe some upper divergence over the southern Blue\nRidge from Roanoke to Boone and surrounding areas.\n\nStrong to occasionally severe thunderstorms may produce\nisolated damaging wind gusts through this evening, but the\nbetter focus for this has shifted to along/south of the VA/NC\nborder.\n\nLike Sunday, outflow-dominant storm mode is likely given that \ndeep-layer flow remains weak. However, 20-25 kts of mid-level \nflow may help in marginal storm organization across the region.\nIn conjunction with steepening low-level lapse rates, a \nmoderately to strongly unstable environment, and high PW, this \nwill support a risk for water-loaded downbursts capable of \noccasionally damaging wind gusts. \n\nTuesday, an upper trough will be oriented over the region, with\nseveral impulses traversing through the it. A trailing cold \nfront will move across much of the Midwest through the period, \nbecoming positioned from western PA to TN and northern AR/OK by \nWednesday morning. Ahead of the main surface front, surface \nprefrontal troughing is expected to develop in the Mid- \nAtlantic vicinity. Deep boundary-layer moisture will overspread\nmuch of the area, with dewpoints from the mid-60s to low 70s. \nThis will aid in development of moderate to strong instability.\n\n\nDeep-layer flow will be strong for this time of year, with 40-60 kt\nflow at 500 mb, and 30-50 kt in the 850-700 mb layer, especially\nalong/north of I-64. Forecast soundings indicate supercell wind\nprofiles with favorably curved low-level hodographs becoming \nelongated above 2-3 km across much of the region. Convective \nevolution remains a bit uncertain, but a mix of supercells and \none or more bowing linear segments are expected across the Ohio \nValley into the Mid-Atlantic. 65+ kt gusts could occur if a \nwell-organized bowing structure and stronger cold pool can \ndevelop. Given warm/moist low-levels and limited low- level \nlapse rates, confidence in this scenario is low. So in general\ndamaging winds seem to be main threat as usual for our area,\nwith isolated tornado potential especially across our northern\nCWA along I-64. Hail cannot be ruled out in we get any\nsupercells, but would be marginally severe sizes.\n\n\nOn Wednesday, the aforementioned front looks to get hung up in a\neast/west orientation along the VA/NC border providing a focal\npoint for shower/thunderstorm activity primarily south of 460.\nSpecifics on severity and impacts expected will come with future\nupdates. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Temperatures should turn more seasonable by the\nmiddle of the week after a frontal passage.\n\nStrong summertime front passing through the region Tuesday night \nwill bring a large pattern change to the area starting \nWednesday. Behind the passage of the front, upper level\ntroughing looks to settle across the eastern conus through much\nof the work week. This will keep 500mb heights much lower across\nthe region leading to overall cooler temperatures. Surface high\npressure coming out of Canada will also lower dewpoints into the\n50s and 60s which will make it feel much more comfortable\noutside during the peak heat of the day. Highs look to only\nclimb into the upper 70s to low 80s across the mountains, and\nmid to upper 80s across the Piedmont starting Wednesday, and\ncontinuing through Saturday before temperatures look to increase\nagain on Sunday of next week. Shower and thunderstorm chances\nbeyond the frontal passage Tuesday night will primarily be\nrelegated to mountain locations, and look to remain isolated, \nwhere orographical lift can enhance development during peak \nheating.",
